DONE SINCE 0.9.1
================

- Unit tests. 'make test' runs tests/run.sh (one case per documented
  behaviour and per bug that was once real), tests/api.c (the library entry
  points, linked directly), and tests/oracle.py (brute-forces the alphabet
  and compares whole sets against Python's re). 'make test-asan' repeats all
  of it under AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and
  LeakSanitizer, with leak detection on.
- Flag to change the enumeration direction: (?L), (?-L) and -L.
- Keyed permutation of the enumeration order: -k key, and the
  rxe_permutation_* entry points in rxe.h.
- Infinite sets. '*', '+' and '{n,}' used to be a hard error; they are now
  enumerated and addressed by index like anything else, with only the
  counting reporting that there is no count. One unbounded quantifier is the
  repeat counts walked without an upper bound; several are several dimensions
  with no largest value, and the index is spread across them by the Cantor
  pairing function. Infinite alternations are dovetailed above the finite
  ones. rxe_is_infinite() is the new entry point. Repeating something that is
  itself unbounded is still refused -- see below.
- Closed-form counted repetition. "x{r0,r1}" is now a repeat count and one
  index per position rather than one written-out copy per position per repeat
  count, so the memory a repetition costs is the length of the string it
  generates instead of the square of the largest repeat count.
  [a-z]{1,20000} needed 26GB and was killed by the OOM killer; it now parses
  in 3MB, and [a-z]{1,1000000} in 25MB. '?' is built by the same routine as
  {0,1} instead of having its own copy of the construction.
- make install now installs librxe.a and rxe.h, so the library can actually
  be linked against.
- Fifteen correctness fixes, from an uninitialised status byte that made
  every parse report a garbage error, through a double free on any
  backreference, to backreference numbering being off by one after every
  (?:...) group. See the git log.


SHORT TERM TO-DOs
=================

- Write API documentation. rxe.h has the data structures commented but
  there is no account of the calling sequence: parse, seek, current,
  iterate, free. tests/api.c is currently the closest thing to one.
- Implement missing things: POSIX character classes, \o, \g with negative
  and named backreferences, \k, etc.
- Decide the caseless backreference question. '(?i)(a)\1' generates
  {aa, AA}, while Perl's matcher accepts {aa, aA, Aa, AA}, since under /i a
  backreference compares case-insensitively. Exact copies are probably the
  more useful reading for generation, but the divergence should either be
  fixed or written down in the manual page.
- rxenum still truncates a generated string at MAXSTRLEN, now 2048 rather
  than 100, and still says nothing about having done so. Nothing sensible
  reaches that, but a wide repetition can: 'a{1,100000}' is counted and
  seeked correctly and then printed wrong past the 2048th element. Either
  size the buffer from the expression or say when the answer was cut.
- Error handling
  > More descriptive error messages pointing to the probable location.
    This needs no structural change: str is in scope at all twenty-seven
    places an error is raised, so it only wants a field for the offset and a
    helper that sets status and position together. Three things to watch.
    rxe_parse skips a leading ^, so offsets must be relative to the original
    argument or the caret sits one character off. Where a nested parse
    propagates its status upward, the inner position has to win, or every
    error inside a group reports the position of the group. And several
    helpers signal failure by returning NULL and leave the caller to decide
    which status that meant, so the place that knows where the trouble was
    is not the place that records what it was. parse_repeat_params now takes
    the status by pointer instead, which is the shape the rest should follow;
    it used to be the worst of them, returning the code inside the mpz_t it
    had been given for the result.


LONG-TERM TO-DOs
================

> Repeat an expression that is itself unbounded. '(a*)*' is refused, and so
  is '(a*){2}' and every other repetition of an endless thing bar zero
  repetitions. It matters more than it looks: '(\d+,)*', a comma-separated
  list, is exactly this shape and is a thing people write.

  Two problems, both of which the finite case never had to answer. Every
  position of the repetition becomes a dimension of its own rather than a
  digit, so a repetition of n copies needs an n-way pairing, and n varies
  with the repeat count. And the block walk that finds the repeat count works
  by stepping past each block in turn, which needs the blocks to be finite;
  here every block from the first onwards is endless, so the count itself has
  to be paired with the index within it rather than subtracted off.

  Both are the machinery that is already there -- rxe_unpair over a varying
  arity, and a pairing rather than a subtraction to pick the count -- so this
  is a bounded piece of work rather than a new idea. The awkward part is the
  block for count zero, which holds exactly one string where every other
  block holds infinitely many, so it is not a copy of N and cannot take part
  in a pairing over N without leaving a hole.

> A stricter shortlex order. The Cantor pairing walks diagonals over the
  dimensions' indices, which coincides with shortest-string-first exactly
  when each index is the length it contributes: 'a*b*' comes out "", a, b,
  aa, ab, bb, which is shortlex. When the repeated unit has more than one
  member -- '[ab]*[cd]*' -- an index of n stands for a string of about log(n)
  characters, so the diagonals slant across the lengths instead of following
  them.

  Getting true shortlex in general means ordering by string length, which
  means being able to say how many members an expression has of each length
  rather than in total. That is a generating function per subexpression
  rather than a single cardinality, and for a finite expression it is
  strictly more expensive than what is there now: '[a-z]{1,20000}' has one
  cardinality of 28,300 digits and would have 20,001 of them, one per length.
  So it would want to be an alternative representation chosen when an
  unbounded quantifier is present, not a replacement. Probably not worth it
  unless somebody actually needs the order.

> Abstract the arithmetic behind a base-type shim, so the width can be
  chosen at build time: GMP for unbounded cardinalities as now, or
  uint64_t / uint32_t for environments that cannot carry a bignum library.
  That makes librxe dependency-free in its fixed-width form -- GMP is
  currently the only dependency -- and puts it within reach of a
  microcontroller. It is also the same abstraction the GPU work needs, a
  kernel having to use fixed-width arithmetic regardless, so the two are one
  mechanism rather than two.

  Cheaper than it sounds. The library core uses only sixteen distinct mpz
  operations (set, set_ui, init, clear, sgn, mul, add, add_ui, sub, tdiv_qr,
  pow_ui, cmp, cmp_ui, get_ui, init_set, init_set_ui), and never assigns one
  mpz_t to another: because mpz_t is an array type, GMP forced every
  operation through a function call, which is exactly the style a swappable
  base type requires. The port is largely mechanical renaming. The demo
  program needs more than the library does -- formatting, random selection,
  decimal parsing -- and would keep requiring GMP, or grow its own
  fixed-width paths.

  The real design question is overflow, not the wrappers. With GMP a
  cardinality cannot overflow; with uint64_t it silently wraps, and the
  threshold is lower than intuition suggests:

      [A-Z]{13}         2,481,152,873,203,736,576   fits
      [A-Z]{14}        64,509,974,703,297,150,976   overflows
      [0-9A-Za-z]{10}     839,299,365,868,340,224   fits
      [0-9A-Za-z]{11}  52,036,560,683,837,093,888   overflows

  So thirteen upper-case letters fit in 64 bits and fourteen do not. The
  shim has to saturate, or detect and report, rather than wrap; deciding
  which, and where the check goes, is the work.

> JIT compiler, and a GPU back end. The parse tree is the intermediate
  representation and a code generator consumes it, so neither requires the
  tree to change. The closed-form repetition rework has happened, which
  removes the reason to wait: a JIT would have had to compile the two
  million nodes a{1,2000} used to expand into, and now compiles one.

  The GPU case has a shape the JIT case does not. A GPU wants independent
  work per thread, and rxe_iterate carries sequentially, so it does not
  parallelise. rxe_seek is per-index and independent: one thread per index
  is the natural kernel, and composes with the keyed permutation above as
  seek(pi(base + t)). The random-access formulation the library already has
  is what makes this possible at all.

  Keep it in-tree behind a build flag until the tree representation settles.
  The structures are already visible in rxe.h, but there is no documented
  traversal contract and no stability promise, and a separate consumer would
  be chasing a moving target through the repetition rework.

> Binary tree over alternations, to replace the linear scan in rxe_seek.
  Deferred, and now probably not worth doing at all: 20,000 hand-written
  alternatives already parse and seek in 0.02s, and enumerate in full in
  0.02s. The one place large alternation counts used to arise in practice
  was handle_repeats, which built one alternation per repeat count, and the
  closed-form rework has removed them. Revisit only if a profile ever says
  otherwise.
